@page "/access-denied"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

@*
    Where a signed-in person with no VMS role lands.

    It exists because the framework's default AccessDeniedPath is
    /MicrosoftIdentity/Account/AccessDenied, and Microsoft.Identity.Web.UI does not
    implement that action - so the one failure this deployment was always going to hit
    first arrived as a bare 404 with no hint that a role was the problem.

    A missing role is not an error in the application. It is a directory task, and the page
    says which one, names the account it is talking about, and says who does it - because
    the person reading it is standing at a reception desk and cannot fix it themselves.
*@

<PageTitle>No access &middot; DI VMS</PageTitle>

<h1>You are signed in, but not yet allowed in</h1>
<p class="page-sub">Your account has no visitor management role</p>

<section class="card">
    <div class="prose">
        <AuthorizeView>
            <Authorized>
                <p>
                    Signed in as <strong>@context.User.Identity?.Name</strong>. Sign-in worked;
                    what is missing is the role that says what you may do here.
                </p>
            </Authorized>
            <NotAuthorized>
                <p>
                    You are not signed in. <a href="/">Go back to the desk</a> to sign in again.
                </p>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeView>

        <h2>What IT needs to do</h2>
        <p>
            In Entra ID, on the <strong>TS-AI-VisitorManagementSystem</strong> application,
            assign one of these app roles to the account above:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Vms.Officer</strong> — check visitors in. This is the one a reception desk needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Vms.Supervisor</strong> — the same, and read the visitor report.</li>
            <li><strong>Vms.Admin</strong> / <strong>Vms.SystemAdmin</strong> — the same again, for administrators.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The roles are declared under <em>App registrations → App roles</em> and handed out
            under <em>Enterprise applications → Users and groups</em>. Both steps are needed:
            a role that exists but is assigned to nobody leaves every account on this page.
        </p>

        <h2>After the role is assigned</h2>
        <p>
            Sign out and in again. The role travels in the sign-in token, so a session that
            started before the assignment will not have it however long you wait.
        </p>

        <p>
            <a class="button" href="/MicrosoftIdentity/Account/SignOut">Sign out</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
